+ * OPAQUE. `formatted_body` is accepted for the comparison only when it is plain Markdown-renderer HTML. Anything else
+ * makes the message OPAQUE: `canonHtml` (or `canonMd`, for the Markdown-side conditions) returns `null`, "no tree", and a
+ * null on either side of `canonEqual` is no match. The copy then takes the converter. Element displays `sanitize-html`
+ * output, whose tokenizer and allow-list differ from an HTML5 parse exactly in what is excluded here.
+ * - v2.2 B1 to B4: the rule has its OWN tokenizer and tree builder, with no DOM and no DOMParser. A string that fits
+ *   the grammar gets the same tree from every HTML5 parser, from htmlparser2 and from a port, so the reconstruction
+ *   and implied-end-tag recovery of HTML5 (R-1) cannot make the rule see a tree Element does not display. B1: strict
+ *   tokens (an entity set, quoted attributes only, `<!` and `<?` and U+0000 refused). B2: an attribute allow-list.
+ *   B3: void elements `br hr img`, every other element needs its end tag matching the top of the open elements, at
+ *   most 40 open. B4: a content model for every element (and A2: `ALLOWED`, everything else is opaque).
